From: dresden196 <50474030+dresden196@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] NickelOS: the GPU sandbox lets Mesa ask F_DUPFD_QUERY

NickelOS Unified's Mesa is Gentoo's 26. It asks whether two of its file
descriptors are one open file with fcntl(fd, F_DUPFD_QUERY, fd2) (Linux
6.10), before falling back to kcmp, which the GPU policy already answers
with ENOSYS. The baseline policy's fcntl rule doesn't know the command and
killed the GPU process with SIGSYS: no GPU, and Chrome, with no software
compositing, quit before the login screen (the AMD test PC, build 23i,
2026-10-21; Intel's Mesa takes the same path). The GPU policy now allows
it beside NVIDIA's F_ADD_SEALS: the answer is only about the process's
own descriptors.

diff --git a/sandbox/policy/linux/bpf_gpu_policy_linux.cc b/sandbox/policy/linux/bpf_gpu_policy_linux.cc
index 74333b96d129f..eb74abbdcb6d2 100644
--- a/sandbox/policy/linux/bpf_gpu_policy_linux.cc
+++ b/sandbox/policy/linux/bpf_gpu_policy_linux.cc
@@ -66,7 +66,17 @@ ResultExpr GpuProcessPolicy::EvaluateSyscall(int sysno) const {
       BoolExpr add_seals =
           AllOf(cmd == F_ADD_SEALS, (arg & ~kAllowedMask) == 0);
 
-      return If(add_seals, Allow()).Else(BPFBasePolicy::EvaluateSyscall(sysno));
+      // NickelOS: Mesa (Gentoo's, 26) asks whether two of its descriptors
+      // are one open file with fcntl(fd, F_DUPFD_QUERY, fd2), Linux 6.10,
+      // before falling back to kcmp. The baseline policy doesn't know the
+      // command and killed the GPU process: no GPU, and Chrome, with no
+      // software compositing, quit before the login screen (the AMD test PC,
+      // 2026-10-21). The answer is about the process's own descriptors.
+      constexpr unsigned int kDupfdQuery = 1027;  // F_LINUX_SPECIFIC_BASE + 3
+
+      return If(add_seals, Allow())
+          .ElseIf(cmd == kDupfdQuery, Allow())
+          .Else(BPFBasePolicy::EvaluateSyscall(sysno));
     }
     case __NR_fdatasync:
     case __NR_ftruncate:
